<header class="soc-head"><p class="featured-region">Social ladder · 1727–1894 (from the conquest of Whydah to the French conquest)</p><h1 class="soc-title">Social Hierarchy of the Kingdom of Dahomey</h1><p class="soc-sub">From King to Enslaved</p></header>
<div class="soc-intro"><figure class="soc-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/glele"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/glele.jpg" alt="Glele"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/glele">Glele</a><span class="soc-credit">Valentin Foulquier · Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p class="soc-lede"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-dahomey">Dahomey</a>, the kingdom of the Fon on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abomey">Abomey</a> plateau, ranked its people around the king and his palaces at Abomey. The king, who claimed descent from the royal house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allada">Allada</a>, alone made the kingdom’s offerings to the spirits, received the gifts and tribute of every lineage at the Annual Customs and appointed the chiefs of its villages; each of his great officers had a woman counterpart. The kingdom was organised for constant war. A standing army of men, and a corps of women soldiers, took captives in wars and raids, and the kingdom sold them to European slave traders at Whydah, set them to work on royal plantations or killed them in the Annual Customs. Most free people were farmers who owed tribute and taxes. European visitors documented the kingdom extensively, but their accounts were often written for one side or the other of the debate over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>, and recent historians stress the limits of the king’s power.</p><p class="soc-facts"><span><b>Period</b> 1727–1894 (from the conquest of Whydah to the French conquest)</span><span><b>People</b> c. 200,000–400,000 (est.)</span><span><b>Ranks</b> 9</span><span><b>Profile</b> <a href="/empire/kingdom-of-dahomey">Kingdom of Dahomey</a></span></p></div>
<section class="soc-ladder-sec" aria-labelledby="soc-ladder-t"><div class="soc-ladder-head"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="soc-ladder-t">The ladder</h2><div class="soc-scale" role="group" aria-label="How to draw the ladder"><button type="button" class="soc-scale-btn" data-scale="0" aria-pressed="true">By rank</button><button type="button" class="soc-scale-btn" data-scale="1" aria-pressed="false">To scale</button></div></div><ol class="soc-pyr" data-scale="0"><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:24%;--k:0"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-ruler" href="#rank-1" style="--sw:0.4%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Ahosu</span> <span class="soc-en">(The king)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">One ruler</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:37%;--k:1"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-nobility soc-est" href="#rank-2" style="--sw:0.4%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Great officers of the court</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">c. 0.02–0.1% (est.)</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:49%;--k:2"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-nobility soc-est" href="#rank-3" style="--sw:1%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Chiefs and royal officials</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">c. 0.5–1.5% (est.)</span></a><a class="soc-bar soc-b-religious soc-est" href="#rank-4" style="--sw:1%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Vodun priests and priestesses</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">c. 0.5–2% (est.)</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:62%;--k:3"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-specialists soc-est" href="#rank-5" style="--sw:4%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Soldiers</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">c. 2–5% (est.)</span></a><a class="soc-bar soc-b-specialists soc-est" href="#rank-6" style="--sw:1%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Agojie</span> <span class="soc-en">(Women soldiers)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">c. 0.5–2% (est.)</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:75%;--k:4"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-specialists soc-est" href="#rank-7" style="--sw:4%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Traders and craftsmen</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">c. 2–5% (est.)</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:87%;--k:5"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-commoners soc-est" href="#rank-8" style="--sw:72%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Free farmers</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">About 72% (the rest, est.)</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:100%;--k:6"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-enslaved soc-est" href="#rank-9" style="--sw:18%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Enslaved people</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">c. 10–25% (est.)</span></a></li></ol><p class="soc-scale-note">By rank, the ladder is drawn as a pyramid. To scale, each bar is as wide as that rank&#8217;s share of the people. Shares marked est. are estimates where no count survives; their boxes and bars are dashed.</p></section>
<button type="button" class="kd-story soc-kd-open">Read the whole story</button>
<section class="soc-ranks" aria-label="The ranks, from the top"><article class="soc-rank soc-b-ruler" id="rank-1"><a class="soc-rank-pic" href="/encyclopedia/ghezo" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ghezo.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></a><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Ruler</span> · <span>One ruler</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Ahosu</span> <span class="soc-en">(The king)</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">The king of Dahomey lived at Abomey, where each king built his own palace with pictures on its walls.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">The king, the ahosu, was the sovereign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-dahomey">Dahomey</a>, and all the kings claimed descent from the royal family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allada">Allada</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/houegbadja">Houegbadja</a> (c. 1645–1685) built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-palaces-of-abomey">Royal Palaces of Abomey</a> and made offerings to the spirits the king’s alone, and he, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akaba">Akaba</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agaja">Agaja</a> laid down much of the succession and the administration. At the Annual Customs the king honoured his ancestors, received the tribute of every lineage, displayed it and gave much of it back, reviewed his troops and closed the debates of the Great Council by declaring its consensus. From <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tegbesu">Tegbesu</a>’s reign (1740–1774) the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> gave him the largest part of his income. Each king built his own palace, its clay reliefs recording his deeds.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Kingship passed through the male line, usually but not always to the eldest son, and the Great Council’s discussions largely decided the choice: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tegbesu">Tegbesu</a>, not the eldest, won a succession struggle with a brother in 1740, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghezo">Ghezo</a> came to the throne in 1818 in place of his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adandozan">Adandozan</a>.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> One man, with the royal family.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/houegbadja">Houegbadja</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agaja">Agaja</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tegbesu">Tegbesu</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghezo">Ghezo</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glele">Glele</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/behanzin">Béhanzin</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-palaces-of-abomey">Royal Palaces of Abomey</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abomey">Abomey</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-nobility" id="rank-2"><a class="soc-rank-pic" href="/encyclopedia/royal-palaces-of-abomey" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/royal-palaces-of-abomey.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></a><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Nobility and officials</span> · <span>c. 0.02–0.1% (est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Great officers of the court</span></h3><p class="soc-term">migan, mehu, yovogan, tokpo, agan, kpojito, chacha</p><p class="soc-kid">The king’s chief ministers ran the courts, the palaces, farming and the army, and each had a woman partner.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">The king’s court was led by great officers. The migan was the prime minister, the king’s chief counsellor, a leading judge and the head executioner. The mehu managed the palaces and the affairs of the royal family, economic matters and the lands south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allada">Allada</a>, and so the dealings with Europeans. Beside them stood the yovogan, the tokpo (minister of agriculture), the agan (general of the army) and the kpojito, the queen mother, and in the 19th century the chacha, the viceroy of Whydah, who took over the work of the king’s traders there with authority over the European traders. In war the migan commanded the right of the army and the mehu the left. Every office but the kpojito’s was held by a man, and each had a female counterpart.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> The kpojito owed her place to her son: Hwanjile, a wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agaja">Agaja</a>, became the first significant kpojito when her son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tegbesu">Tegbesu</a> was king. The chacha’s title went to Francisco Félix de Sousa, a former enslaved man turned slave trader, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghezo">Ghezo</a> came to the throne.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Estimate: some seven great offices, each with its female counterpart, and their households, among perhaps 200,000–400,000 people.</p></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-nobility" id="rank-3"><span class="soc-rank-pic" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/dahomey-3.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Nobility and officials</span> · <span>c. 0.5–1.5% (est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Chiefs and royal officials</span></h3><p class="soc-term">tokpe (tax officials)</p><p class="soc-kid">The king chose chiefs to run each village and officials to count the harvest and collect taxes.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-dahomey">Dahomey</a>’s main political divisions were its villages, whose chiefs and officials the king appointed to act as his representatives and settle disputes. Tax officials, the tokpe, gathered figures on each village’s harvest, and the king set its tax by its production and population. Toll booths on the kingdom’s roads took yearly taxes on the goods people carried and on their occupations; their officials had armed guards and sometimes fined travellers for public nuisance before letting them pass. Courts presided over by officials the government recognised could sit anywhere, in the market or on the road, and take a payment from the litigants before judging. At the Annual Customs officials pressed petitions, debated in the Great Council, where low-ranking officials could publicly rebuke high-ranking ones, and spent their evenings building coalitions.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Chiefs and officials held their posts by the king’s appointment, and at the Annual Customs they received gifts for their services.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Estimate: a chief for each village and the tax and toll officials, with their households.</p></div><p class="soc-pic-cr">Picture: A récade, a sceptre of the kingdom of Dahomey (Muséum de Toulouse) · Didier Descouens · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:R%C3%A9cade_MHNT_ETH_AC_450.jpg" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></p></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-religious" id="rank-4"><span class="soc-rank-pic" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/dahomey-4.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Religious and learned</span> · <span>c. 0.5–2% (est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Vodun priests and priestesses</span></h3><p class="soc-term">-</p><p class="soc-kid">Priests and priestesses looked after the shrines of Dahomey’s many gods.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-dahomey">Dahomey</a>’s religion joined older animist traditions to Vodun. The paired god Mawu-Lisa, with female and male natures, ruled the sky and the highest pantheon, and other gods lived in the earth and in thunder. Each god and each pantheon had its own priesthood and shrines; women made up a significant part of the priests, and the chief priest was always a descendant of Dakodonu, counted as the kingdom’s second king. The kings drew worship to themselves. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/houegbadja">Houegbadja</a> made offerings to the spirits the king’s to give, and oral tradition holds that Hwanjile, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agaja">Agaja</a>’s wife and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tegbesu">Tegbesu</a>’s mother, brought Vodun to the kingdom, created two new gods and tied worship more closely to the king. Families could hold their own lineage ceremonies only after the royal Customs were over.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> The sources say little about how priests were made, except that the chief priest had to be of Dakodonu’s line.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Estimate: a priesthood and shrines for each god, with many women among the priests.</p></div><p class="soc-pic-cr">Picture: The Temple of the Pythons at Ouidah, a Vodun shrine · Ji-Elle · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Entr%C3%A9e_du_Temple_des_Pythons_(Ouidah).jpg" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></p></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-specialists" id="rank-5"><span class="soc-rank-pic" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/dahomey-5.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Soldiers, merchants and craftsmen</span> · <span>c. 2–5% (est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Soldiers</span></h3><p class="soc-term">-</p><p class="soc-kid">Dahomey’s soldiers trained from boyhood and were rewarded with cowrie shells.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">From at least the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agaja">Agaja</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-dahomey">Dahomey</a> kept a professional standing army, encamped wherever the king was: about 10,000 strong by one account, 15,000 by another, divided into right, left, centre and reserve and into companies and platoons. Boys were recruited at seven or eight as shield carriers for the regular soldiers and joined the ranks after years of apprenticeship. Each soldier was paid a bonus in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cowrie-shell">cowrie</a> shells for every enemy he killed or captured. They fought with European flintlock <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">muskets</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> swords and cutlasses, were drilled constantly and fired on command, and by the late 19th century the kingdom had a large arsenal of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/breech-loading-rifle">breech-loading rifles</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abeokuta">Abeokuta</a> inflicted crushing defeats on them in the 19th century, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> defeated them in 1890 and 1892–1894.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Boys entered at seven or eight as shield carriers and rose by service and by the captives they took; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghezo">Ghezo</a> also recruited soldiers from foreign captives.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Estimate: a standing army of some 10,000–15,000 under Agaja; with the women a third of the army in the mid-19th century, the men numbered perhaps 2,000–12,000, among some 200,000–400,000 people.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cowrie-shell">Cowrie shell</a></div></div><p class="soc-pic-cr">Picture: The battle of Atchoukpa (1890), by Marcel Vagné, about 1900 · Marcel Vagné (1842-1901) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Atchoukpa.png" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></p></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-specialists" id="rank-6"><a class="soc-rank-pic" href="/encyclopedia/dahomey-amazons" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dahomey-amazons.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></a><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Soldiers, merchants and craftsmen</span> · <span>c. 0.5–2% (est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Agojie</span> <span class="soc-en">(Women soldiers)</span></h3><p class="soc-term">Mino, ahosi (king's wives)</p><p class="soc-kid">Dahomey had an army of women soldiers who trained very hard and were respected in the kingdom.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">The women soldiers were called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dahomey-amazons">Agojie</a> or Mino (“our mothers”) and called themselves ahosi, the king’s wives; Europeans named them after the Amazons of Greek myth. Their origins are debated: a corps of elephant hunters, the gbeto, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/houegbadja">Houegbadja</a>; a female bodyguard; or women sent around 1729 to make the army look larger, who fought so bravely that they became a permanent corps. Many of the first were criminals pressed into service instead of death. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghezo">Ghezo</a> ordered every family to send him its daughters and chose the fittest. Legally married to the king, they could not bear children or live a married life. By the mid-19th century they numbered 1,000–6,000, about a third of the army, under women commanders; they were wealthy, held high status, could rise to command and took a prominent part in the Great Council.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Women entered by capture, by the king’s levy of daughters, as a punishment or of their own will, and could rise to command; the corps was largely wiped out at Adegon in 1892 and disbanded in 1894.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Estimate: visitors’ reports put the women soldiers at 1,000–6,000 in the mid-19th century, among a population that is itself an estimate.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dahomey-amazons">Dahomey Amazons</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-specialists" id="rank-7"><span class="soc-rank-pic" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/dahomey-7.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Soldiers, merchants and craftsmen</span> · <span>c. 2–5% (est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Traders and craftsmen</span></h3><p class="soc-term">akhigan (king's traders), akueho (cowrie huts)</p><p class="soc-kid">Craftspeople in Dahomey made carvings, brass figures and colourful cloth pictures, and traders sold goods in busy markets.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">Craftwork was dominated by a formal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a> system, and blacksmiths, weavers and wood cutters paid taxes on their work. The kings paid artists well, and commoners as well as royals made important works: carvings, appliqué cloth, clay reliefs and small brass figures that the well-off wore or kept at home. Markets turned on a four-day cycle, a different market each day. At Whydah the king’s own traders, the akhigan, traded for him and collected customs, and private traders needed his licence. Wealthy citizens kept their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cowrie-shell">cowries</a> in fire- and theft-proof huts, the akueho, in their compounds, and held others’ deposits there. In the 19th century private traders were at first the sole owners of the large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palm-oil">palm oil</a> plantations, until the aristocracy joined them from the mid-century.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Trade made fortunes, but at the king’s pleasure, since a private trader needed his licence to trade at Whydah; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palm-oil">palm oil</a> plantations of the 19th century opened a new road to wealth.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Estimate: the guild craftsmen, the licensed traders of Whydah and the markets, and the wealthy who kept cowrie stores, with their households.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palm-oil">Palm oil</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cowrie-shell">Cowrie shell</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ouidah">Ouidah</a></div></div><p class="soc-pic-cr">Picture: Fon appliqué cloth with the emblems of the kings of Dahomey · IvanaMadzarevic · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Afri%C4%8Dka_kraljevska_tkanina_naroda_Fon_(Benin).jpg" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></p></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-commoners" id="rank-8"><span class="soc-rank-pic" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/dahomey-8.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Free commoners</span> · <span>About 72% (the rest, est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Free farmers</span></h3><p class="soc-term">-</p><p class="soc-kid">Most people in Dahomey were farmers who brought gifts to the king at a great yearly festival.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">Most people were free farmers and their families: farming was done by most families, for local use until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palm-oil">palm oil</a> was exported. Every lineage owed gifts and tribute to the king at the Annual Customs, which most people were required to attend, and the king displayed the riches and gave much of them back. Villages were taxed on their harvests, and people paid tolls on the roads according to their goods and their trade. The Great Council at the Customs brought together a large part of the population, women as well as men. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adandozan">Adandozan</a> Dahomeans themselves were sold into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghezo">Ghezo</a> ended that and sold only captured enemies, and he also abolished the death penalty for some lesser offences, such as adultery.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> A family’s sons could enter the army as boys and its daughters could be levied for the women’s corps; criminals could be pressed into the corps or killed at the Annual Customs.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Everyone not counted in the other ranks: the great majority, most of them farming families, Fon on the Abomey plateau and other peoples in the conquered lands. The other ranks are estimates, so this share is one too.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fon-people">Fon people</a></div></div><p class="soc-pic-cr">Picture: A Fon village in Dahomey, about 1930 · Jean Duprez · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Village_traditionnel_de_l'ethnie_Fon_au_Dahomey_-_Jean_Duprez_-_btv1b101110693.jpg" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></p></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-enslaved" id="rank-9"><span class="soc-rank-pic" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/dahomey-9.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Enslaved</span> · <span>c. 10–25% (est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Enslaved people</span></h3><p class="soc-term">-</p><p class="soc-kid">Dahomey captured people in wars and sold many of them as slaves to European traders.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-dahomey">Dahomey</a>’s growth went with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a>. Its armies took men, women and children in wars and raids and sold them at Whydah to European traders for guns, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a>, cloth, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cowrie-shell">cowries</a> and alcohol; before the sale captives were made to march in circles round the “Tree of Forgetfulness”, so that they would forget their homes and their spirits would not return for revenge. Captives kept in Dahomey worked the royal plantations that fed the army and the court, and from the mid-19th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palm-oil">palm oil</a> plantations. Captives and criminals were killed at the Annual Customs, some 500 a year, figures that pro-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> writers often exaggerated. The trade outlasted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>’s treaty of 1852: in 1860 the Clotilda carried 110 captives from Whydah to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a>, among them Oluale Kossola.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Watson and Schellinger argue that slaves were once treated as members of their master’s family and could become free within a generation or two, but that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palm-oil">palm oil</a> plantations made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> harsher; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghezo">Ghezo</a> had the murders of slaves tried at the court in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abomey">Abomey</a>.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Estimate: captives worked the royal plantations that fed the army and the court, and in the 19th century the palm oil plantations; in 1860 an American slaving captain was shown a warehouse holding 4,000 captives.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">Slavery</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/human-sacrifice">Human sacrifice</a></div></div><p class="soc-pic-cr">Picture: The Door of No Return at Ouidah, a memorial to the captives shipped from the port · Borisghost · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Porte_du_non-retour_au_Benin.jpg" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></p></div></article></section>
<section class="soc-born" id="born" aria-labelledby="soc-born-t"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="soc-born-t">Born into history</h2><p class="soc-born-lede">Where would you have stood? Chance places you on the ladder, as birth placed most people: weighted by how many each rank held.</p><button type="button" class="soc-born-btn" data-born="dahomey">Be born in the Kingdom of Dahomey</button><div class="soc-born-out" aria-live="polite"></div></section>
<section class="soc-sec"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Moving up and down</h2><p>Office in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-dahomey">Dahomey</a> came from the king, and war was the main road up and the main road down. Soldiers earned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cowrie-shell">cowries</a> for every captive, women soldiers could rise to command, wealth and high status, and officials received gifts at the Annual Customs and built coalitions there. A king’s mother could become one of the great officers, as Hwanjile did, and royal favour could raise a stranger: Francisco Félix de Sousa, once enslaved himself, became a major slave trader, the chacha of Whydah and holder of the monopoly on the export of slaves after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghezo">Ghezo</a> came to the throne. The throne itself was contested: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tegbesu">Tegbesu</a> won it in a struggle with a brother, and Ghezo replaced his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adandozan">Adandozan</a>. At the bottom the 19th century hardened the line. On one view, slaves had once been able to win free status after a generation or two, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palm-oil">palm oil</a> plantations made their lives harsher, and the captives killed at the Customs or sold at Whydah had no way out. The kingdom fell to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1894, and in 1904 it became part of the colony of French Dahomey.</p></section>
<section class="soc-sec"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Women on the ladder</h2><p>Women had a place in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-dahomey">Dahomey</a>’s government that set it apart: each great office held by a man had a female counterpart, the kpojito, the queen mother, ranked among the great officers, and women sat in the Great Council. Hwanjile, the first significant kpojito, is credited with reshaping the kingdom’s religion, and women made up a significant part of the priests. Tradition names Hangbe, a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/houegbadja">Houegbadja</a>, as ruler from 1716 to 1718, though some sources doubt that she existed. The king’s wives, the ahosi, often numbered in the hundreds, and the women soldiers came from among them or were levied from the families of the kingdom; a woman could also be enrolled against her will if her husband or father complained of her behaviour. Prostitution was licensed by the king: from the 18th century the government placed prostitutes about the kingdom at a price set by decree and taxed them at the Customs. Enslaved women were sold with men and children, and in 1849 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghezo">Ghezo</a> gave a captive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> girl, later named Sarah Forbes Bonetta, to the British naval officer Frederick Forbes as a “gift”.</p></section>
