<section class="soc-sec"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Sources and further reading</h2><ul class="essay-refs"><li>Alpern, S. B. (1998) Amazons of Black Sparta: The Women Warriors of Dahomey. New York: New York University Press.</li><li>Bay, E. G. (1998) Wives of the Leopard: Gender, Politics, and Culture in the Kingdom of Dahomey. Charlottesville: University of Virginia Press.</li><li>Forbes, F. E. (1851) Dahomey and the Dahomans, Being the Journals of Two Missions to the King of Dahomey and the Residence at his Capital in the Years 1849 and 1850. London: Longman, Brown, Green, and Longmans.</li><li>Law, R. (1993) &quot;The 'Amazons' of Dahomey&quot;. Paideuma 39: 245–260.</li><li>Law, R. (2004) Ouidah: The Social History of a West African Slaving 'Port', 1727–1892. Athens: Ohio University Press.</li><li>Yoder, J. C. (1974) &quot;Fly and Elephant Parties: Political Polarization in Dahomey, 1840–1870&quot;. Journal of African History 15 (3): 417–432.</li></ul><p class="soc-method">The shares are of the whole population, children included. Where a scholar has counted or estimated a rank, its share follows that work, named on the rank; where no count survives, the share is an estimate, marked est. and drawn dashed, made from what the rank was and did, from related figures and from the same rank in comparable societies, and its note says what it rests on. Population: Estimate: the sources give no figure; a standing army of some 10,000–15,000 under Agaja, and in the mid-19th century 1,000–6,000 women soldiers making up about a third of the army, suggest a kingdom of a few hundred thousand people.</p></section>
