<script type="application/json" id="soc-data">{"slug":"dahomey","name":"The Kingdom of Dahomey","title":"Social Hierarchy of the Kingdom of Dahomey: From King to Enslaved","of":"the Kingdom of Dahomey","short":"Dahomey","in":"the Kingdom of Dahomey","year":1810,"yl":"1810","period":"1727–1894 (from the conquest of Whydah to the French conquest)","region":"Sub-Saharan Africa","pop":[200000,400000],"popEst":1,"img":"/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/glele.jpg","tiers":[{"n":1,"lv":1,"band":"ruler","name":"The king","loc":"Ahosu","term":"","lbl":"One ruler","w":0,"firm":1,"pic":"/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ghezo.jpg","you":"You are the ahosu, heir of the kings of Allada; each year at the Annual Customs you receive the tribute of every lineage, honour your ancestors and close the Great Council’s debates by declaring its consensus.","kid":"The king of Dahomey lived at Abomey, where each king built his own palace with pictures on its walls.","rise":"Kingship passed through the male line, usually but not always to the eldest son, and the Great Council’s discussions largely decided the choice: Tegbesu, not the eldest, won a succession struggle with a brother in 1740, and Ghezo came to the throne in 1818 in place of his brother Adandozan.","why":"The king, the ahosu, was the sovereign of Dahomey, and all the kings claimed descent from the royal family of Allada.","people":[["houegbadja","Houegbadja"],["agaja","Agaja"],["tegbesu","Tegbesu"],["ghezo","Ghezo"],["glele","Glele"],["behanzin","Béhanzin"]]},{"n":2,"lv":2,"band":"nobility","name":"Great officers of the court","term":"migan, mehu, yovogan, tokpo, agan, kpojito, chacha","lbl":"c. 0.02–0.1% (est.)","w":0.06,"firm":1,"est":1,"pic":"/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/royal-palaces-of-abomey.jpg","you":"You are the migan, the king’s chief counsellor and a leading judge, and his head executioner; in war you command the right of the army, and a woman holds your office’s counterpart.","kid":"The king’s chief ministers ran the courts, the palaces, farming and the army, and each had a woman partner.","rise":"The kpojito owed her place to her son: Hwanjile, a wife of Agaja, became the first significant kpojito when her son Tegbesu was king. The chacha’s title went to Francisco Félix de Sousa, a former enslaved man turned slave trader, after Ghezo came to the throne.","why":"The king’s court was led by great officers.","people":[]},{"n":3,"lv":3,"band":"nobility","name":"Chiefs and royal officials","term":"tokpe (tax officials)","lbl":"c. 0.5–1.5% (est.)","w":1,"firm":1,"est":1,"pic":"/images/societies/thumbs/dahomey-3.jpg","you":"The king appointed you chief of your village; you settle its disputes, the tax officials count its harvest, and each year you go to Abomey for the Customs and the Great Council.","kid":"The king chose chiefs to run each village and officials to count the harvest and collect taxes.","rise":"Chiefs and officials held their posts by the king’s appointment, and at the Annual Customs they received gifts for their services.","why":"Dahomey’s main political divisions were its villages, whose chiefs and officials the king appointed to act as his representatives and settle disputes.","people":[]},{"n":4,"lv":3,"band":"religious","name":"Vodun priests and priestesses","term":"-","lbl":"c. 0.5–2% (est.)","w":1.25,"firm":0,"est":1,"pic":"/images/societies/thumbs/dahomey-4.jpg","you":"You serve at the shrine of one of the gods of thunder, and your own family’s ceremonies for its ancestors must wait until the king’s Annual Customs are over.","kid":"Priests and priestesses looked after the shrines of Dahomey’s many gods.","rise":"The sources say little about how priests were made, except that the chief priest had to be of Dakodonu’s line.","why":"Dahomey’s religion joined older animist traditions to Vodun.","people":[]},{"n":5,"lv":4,"band":"specialists","name":"Soldiers","term":"-","lbl":"c. 2–5% (est.)","w":3.5,"firm":0,"est":1,"pic":"/images/societies/thumbs/dahomey-5.jpg","you":"You joined the army at eight as a shield carrier; now you carry a flintlock in the king’s standing army, and every captive you bring back earns you cowries.","kid":"Dahomey’s soldiers trained from boyhood and were rewarded with cowrie shells.","rise":"Boys entered at seven or eight as shield carriers and rose by service and by the captives they took; Ghezo also recruited soldiers from foreign captives.","why":"From at least the time of Agaja Dahomey kept a professional standing army, encamped wherever the king was: about 10,000 strong by one account, 15,000 by another, divided into right, left, centre and reserve and into…","people":[]},{"n":6,"lv":4,"band":"specialists","name":"Women soldiers","loc":"Agojie","term":"Mino, ahosi (king's wives)","lbl":"c. 0.5–2% (est.)","w":1.25,"firm":0,"est":1,"pic":"/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dahomey-amazons.jpg","you":"You were chosen from the daughters the king required of every family; legally his wife, you may not bear children or live a married life, but you may rise to command and speak in the Great Council.","kid":"Dahomey had an army of women soldiers who trained very hard and were respected in the kingdom.","rise":"Women entered by capture, by the king’s levy of daughters, as a punishment or of their own will, and could rise to command; the corps was largely wiped out at Adegon in 1892 and disbanded in 1894.","why":"The women soldiers were called Agojie or Mino (“our mothers”) and called themselves ahosi, the king’s wives; Europeans named them after the Amazons of Greek myth.","people":[]},{"n":7,"lv":5,"band":"specialists","name":"Traders and craftsmen","term":"akhigan (king's traders), akueho (cowrie huts)","lbl":"c. 2–5% (est.)","w":3.5,"firm":0,"est":1,"pic":"/images/societies/thumbs/dahomey-7.jpg","you":"You trade at Whydah under the king’s licence and keep your cowries, and your neighbours’, in the strong hut in your compound.","kid":"Craftspeople in Dahomey made carvings, brass figures and colourful cloth pictures, and traders sold goods in busy markets.","rise":"Trade made fortunes, but at the king’s pleasure, since a private trader needed his licence to trade at Whydah; the palm oil plantations of the 19th century opened a new road to wealth.","why":"Craftwork was dominated by a formal guild system, and blacksmiths, weavers and wood cutters paid taxes on their work.","people":[]},{"n":8,"lv":6,"band":"commoners","name":"Free farmers","term":"-","lbl":"About 72% (the rest, est.)","w":71.94,"firm":1,"est":1,"pic":"/images/societies/thumbs/dahomey-8.jpg","you":"You farm with your family and bring your lineage’s tribute to the Annual Customs at Abomey, where the king displays it and gives much of it back.","kid":"Most people in Dahomey were farmers who brought gifts to the king at a great yearly festival.","rise":"A family’s sons could enter the army as boys and its daughters could be levied for the women’s corps; criminals could be pressed into the corps or killed at the Annual Customs.","why":"Most people were free farmers and their families: farming was done by most families, for local use until palm oil was exported.","people":[]},{"n":9,"lv":7,"band":"enslaved","name":"Enslaved people","term":"-","lbl":"c. 10–25% (est.)","w":17.5,"firm":1,"est":1,"pic":"/images/societies/thumbs/dahomey-9.jpg","you":"You were captured in a raid on your town; you may be marched round the Tree of Forgetfulness and sold at Whydah, or kept to work the king’s palm oil plantations.","kid":"Dahomey captured people in wars and sold many of them as slaves to European traders.","rise":"Watson and Schellinger argue that slaves were once treated as members of their master’s family and could become free within a generation or two, but that the palm oil plantations made slavery harsher; Ghezo had the murders of slaves tried at the court in Abomey.","why":"Dahomey’s growth went with the Atlantic slave trade.","people":[]}]}</script>
